  "installIphoneTitle": "Install on iPhone",
  "@installIphoneTitle": {
    "description": "iPhone install steps: title"
  },
  "installIphoneSteps": "An iPhone only installs apps from the App Store or signed by an Apple developer, and the release on GitHub isn't signed. Build it on a Mac with Xcode and Flutter instead, signed with your own Apple account (a free one works):\n\n1. Get TabCam's code from GitHub and set your Apple team, as the guide says.\n2. Connect the iPhone to the Mac by USB and turn on Developer Mode on it (Settings → Privacy & Security → Developer Mode).\n3. Build and install the release with the guide's command. It installs over TabCam and keeps your events.\n4. The first time, trust the developer on the iPhone: Settings → General → VPN & Device Management.\n\nWith a free Apple account, the app stops opening after 7 days: install it again.",
  "@installIphoneSteps": {
    "description": "iPhone install steps: how to build and install the app from GitHub"
  },
  "installAndroidTitle": "Install on Android",
  "@installAndroidTitle": {
    "description": "Android install steps: title"
  },
  "installAndroidSteps": "1. On the phone, open TabCam's latest release on GitHub (the button below).\n2. Under Assets, tap the file ending in -android.apk to download it.\n3. Open the downloaded file. The first time, Android asks you to allow your browser to install apps: allow it (Settings → Apps → Special app access → Install unknown apps).\n4. Tap Install, or Update if TabCam is already installed. Updating keeps your events.\n\nGoogle sign-in doesn't work in these builds yet.",
  "@installAndroidSteps": {
    "description": "Android install steps: how to download and install the APK from GitHub"
  },
